\title{\Large What do zoning and adopted plans add to predicting where cities build?\\[0.3em]
\large Evidence from official parcel data in seven US cities}
\author{Parya Payami\\ \small Department of Architecture, University of Wisconsin--Milwaukee}
\date{Draft, \today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent Planning support tools project land-use scenarios by combining what exists, what zoning permits and
what the plan intends, yet what each layer contributes is rarely tested. We assembled the
official existing land use, adopted plan and zoning for @@parcels_total@@ parcels in @@n_cities@@ large US cities,
including a plan published only as PDF maps (digitised with median errors of @@phl_err_lo@@--@@phl_err_hi@@
ft). In @@n_test@@ cities with parcel histories, models fitted on one period were scored on the next, adding one
layer at a time. Existing conditions ranked the lots that developed with an AUC of @@auc_E_rest_lo@@--@@auc_E_hi@@ in
six cities (@@auc_E_lo@@ in the seventh). Context added most: +@@ctx_gain_lo@@ to +@@ctx_gain_hi@@ where significant
(@@n_ctx_sig@@ cities).
Over context, zoning and the plan added at most +@@zp_small_max@@, except in Phoenix (+@@phx_z@@ and +@@phx_p@@), and
two of these gains disappeared when lots rezoned during the study were excluded. Zoning and plan classes predicted
what developed lots became better than their existing use. Capacity build-out exceeded the homes built
@@bo_lo@@- to @@bo_hi@@-fold; likelihood weighting gave @@lk_lo@@--@@lk_hi@@ times the observed total; a five-year
trend ranked districts better. Regulatory layers inform what development becomes and how much it could hold;
existing conditions and context predict where.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} planning support systems; zoning; land-use plans; urban development prediction;
digital twins; model validation

\section{Introduction}

Land-use decisions in US cities rest on projections. Rezonings, amendments to comprehensive plans and housing targets
are assessed by estimating the development they will bring. Planning support systems and scenario tools make these
estimates by overlaying parcel layers (existing land use, zoning and the adopted plan) and, increasingly, a model of
what is likely to happen \citep{klosterman1999,waddell2002,geertman2020}. The common default is capacity: each
eligible site is assumed to build out to what the rules allow. Capacity answers a legal question, what a regulation
permits, transparently. It is also used to answer a different question: what will be built.

There are reasons to expect the two answers to diverge. Development is rare and selective: in the one- and two-year
windows studied here, @@base_lo@@ to @@base_hi@@ of lots developed. Causal studies disagree on whether upzoning
changes construction in the short run. In Chicago, upzoned parcels rose in value without more permitted homes within
five years \citep{freemark2020}, whereas upzoning raised construction in Portland and Auckland
\citep{dong2024,greenaway2023}. Evaluations of plan implementation find that adopted plans are followed only in part
\citep{talen1996,laurian2004,brody2005,berke2006}. In the three Manhattan rezonings replayed below, a capacity screen
that reproduces the environmental reviews' own projections on their projected sites (@@feis_eh@@ and @@feis_sn@@
times the review figures) expected @@g_capacity@@ homes on development sites. By 2025, @@g_observed@@ had been built.

The predictive value of each regulatory layer is nonetheless seldom measured. Land-change models now incorporate
zoning and planning regulations \citep{domingo2021,abolhasani2026}, but they use them to simulate scenarios, not to
test what the regulations add on held-out history. Reviews of planning support systems and urban digital twins find
that claimed capabilities run ahead of demonstrated ones \citep{vonk2005,azadi2025,kruse2026}. The question matters
for practice because zoning and plans are the layers that planners set. If they add little to predicting where
development happens, scenario tools built on them will misjudge where change occurs and how much of it a decision
will bring. If they add much, tools should give them weight.

A practical barrier is data. The three layers are published by different agencies, in different schemas, and some
plans exist only as documents: Philadelphia's land-use plan exists only as maps in @@phl_maps@@ PDF district plans. A
test across cities therefore first requires the layers on one schema, with every translation reported.

This paper makes four contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Data.} The official existing land use, adopted plan and zoning for @@parcels_total@@ parcels in ten of
the largest US cities, on one 16-class schema, with ingestion checks: homes are within
@@homes_ratio_lo@@--@@homes_ratio_hi@@ times the Census 2020 count in every city. It also includes a validated method
to digitise plans published as vector PDF maps.
\item \textbf{Test.} A cross-city, out-of-time component ablation of what zoning and the adopted plan add to existing
conditions and neighbourhood context, in @@n_test@@ cities and @@test_lots@@ lots. It covers three planning questions:
where development happens, what it becomes and how many homes it adds. It controls for layers that postdate the
outcomes.
\item \textbf{Scenario evidence.} Capacity build-out, likelihood-weighted build-out and a simple trend, compared with
what was built in four cities and in three Manhattan rezonings.
\item \textbf{Open implementation.} PlanArena, a parcel-level planning support system that generates every number
reported here from public sources and exports layers and scenarios to ArcGIS Urban.
\end{enumerate}
The results are predictive. They show how much each layer helps to anticipate development, not what zoning or plans
cause. Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work, Section~\ref{sec:data} the data,
Section~\ref{sec:methods} the methods and Section~\ref{sec:results} the results; Section~\ref{sec:discussion}
discusses implications and limits.

\section{Related work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Predicting urban development.} Spatial models of urban growth range from cellular automata calibrated on
historical urbanisation \citep{clarke1997} to site-level statistical models of land-use change \citep{landis1998} and
behavioural simulations of households, firms and developers interacting through land markets
\citep{waddell2002,waddell2003}. Agent-based models represent the decisions behind land change \citep{parker2003}.
Reviews list neighbourhood effects, temporal dynamics and cross-scale interactions as priorities
\citep{verburg2004,brown2013,weerasinghe2025}. At the parcel level, autologistic models capture existing and
historical neighbourhood conditions \citep{tepe2020}, and machine learning and econometric models of redevelopment are
complements rather than substitutes \citep{carruthers2024}. Urban economics ties redevelopment to the value of land
relative to that of the existing structure \citep{rosenthal1994,munneke1996,dye2007}. Parcel studies in Chicago find
that smaller and older buildings with less lot coverage are demolished and replaced more often
\citep{weber2006,charles2013}. These findings motivate the existing-conditions and context features used here.

\paragraph{Regulations and plans as predictors.} Land-use regulation affects new construction \citep{mayer2000}, but
rezoning is not random: in Los Angeles, upzoning occurred where development opportunity met weak political resistance
\citep{gabbe2018}. Current zoning may therefore reflect development that was already coming, which complicates any
test of zoning against past development. Studies of upzoning estimate causal effects in one city at a time and differ
in their conclusions \citep{freemark2020,dong2024,greenaway2023}. Plan evaluation distinguishes conformance, whether
outcomes match the plan, from performance, whether the plan shaped decisions \citep{talen1996,laurian2004,berke2006,
loh2011,feitelson2017}. Land-change science has called for planning to be represented explicitly in models
\citep{hersperger2018}. Two recent studies do so:
\begin{itemize}
\item \citet{domingo2021} use the floor area each zoning plan allows per parcel in multi-scenario simulations of four
Spanish urban areas;
\item \citet{abolhasani2026} standardise local planning regulations for 3D land-use simulation in Zurich and
Copenhagen.
\end{itemize}
Both integrate regulations into simulation; neither measures what the regulations add to prediction on held-out
history.

\paragraph{Planning support systems and urban digital twins.} Planning support systems combine land suitability,
demand projection and allocation in scenario planning \citep{klosterman1999,geertman2020}. Their limited use and
uncertain usefulness are long-standing concerns \citep{vonk2005,tebrommelstroet2013,pelzer2017}. Urban digital twins
extend the idea to integrated city models \citep{batty2018,batty2024,white2021,lehtola2022,ferrebigorra2022}.
Reviews report technical and organisational barriers \citep{lei2023,wu2025} and a gap between the ambitions of urban
twins and their demonstrated contributions to planning \citep{azadi2025,luo2025,kruse2026}. Optimisation and
allocation methods generate and rank land-use alternatives \citep{malczewski2006,rahman2021,zheng2023,feng2024,
lin2025}. This paper asks a prior question: what the regulatory layers on which such methods operate add as evidence.

\paragraph{Validation.} Land-change models should be compared with a null model of persistence, at several
resolutions \citep{pontius2008}, with disagreement split into quantity and allocation \citep{pontius2011}. Their
transition probabilities should be validated directly, not only through simulated maps \citep{black2023}. Under
spatial and temporal dependence, random cross-validation overstates skill \citep{roberts2017,ploton2020}, and a model
applies only where new data resemble its training data \citep{meyer2021}. We therefore test out of time and across
cities \citep{wei2016}, and resample spatial tiles to estimate uncertainty \citep{kunsch1989}.

\paragraph{Map digitisation.} Digital map processing and automatic georeferencing have been developed mainly for
scanned raster maps \citep{chiang2014,luft2021,milleville2022}. Plans exported from GIS as vector PDFs keep each lot as
a filled shape, so the shapes can be read directly and registered to parcels with standard image and point
registration \citep{reddy1996,besl1992,umeyama1991}.

\section{Data}\label{sec:data}

\subsection{Ten cities, three official layers}

The study covers the nine largest US cities other than San Jose, and Milwaukee (Table~\ref{tab:cities}). For each
city, the existing land use is the parcel land use published by the city or its regional planning agency. Where that
layer groups uses, it is refined with assessor records: unit counts split single-family from multi-family housing,
and building classes identify offices, hotels, schools and warehouses. The adopted plan (future land use) and zoning
are separate layers from the same or another official source. Every source code is translated by an explicit
crosswalk into 16 classes, which nest in nine analysis groups:
\begin{itemize}
\item housing: single-family, two to four homes, five or more;
\item mixed use, retail, office, hotel;
\item industry and warehousing;
\item education, health and civic uses;
\item open space, transportation, parking and vacant land.
\end{itemize}
Floor area, homes, year built and assessed value come from assessor or city records. Where records lack floor area,
it is estimated from building footprints and heights and flagged. Jobs are fitted to the Census Bureau's LEHD block
counts. Every input is downloaded by script, with its URL and SHA-256 hash recorded.

@@table_cities@@

Existing use covers at least @@existing_min@@ of parcels in every city. Zoning is available in @@n_with_zoning@@ cities
(Houston has none) and an adopted plan in @@n_with_plan@@. New York, Chicago and Houston publish no citywide land-use
map, and Milwaukee's area plans are PDF documents that could not be retrieved by script. Where both layers exist, the
existing use already lies in the plan's analysis group on @@plan_match_lo@@--@@plan_match_hi@@ of parcels. Plans
mostly confirm what exists; their information lies in the minority of parcels where they differ.

\subsection{Digitising a plan published as PDF maps}

Philadelphia2035's land-use recommendations exist only as ``Proposed Land Use'' maps in its district plans:
@@phl_maps@@ documents covering @@phl_districts@@ districts. The maps are vector drawings in which each lot is a
filled shape in one of the City Planning Commission's categories. We convert them in six steps
(Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}):
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Legend.} The legend is read: each swatch's fill colour, with the label to its right.
\item \textbf{Colours.} Map colours are matched one to one to legend colours by perceptual distance (CIELAB $\Delta
E$, Hungarian assignment), because a legend may be CMYK while the map is RGB. Pairs further apart than $\Delta E =
45$ are not matched.
\item \textbf{Visibility.} Maps often draw a wider area and clip it to the district, and the vector shapes carry no
clipping. Each shape is kept only if the rendered page shows its colour at a point inside it, and only the largest
map frame on the page spread is kept, which removes insets.
\item \textbf{Registration.} A similarity transform (scale, rotation, translation) is fitted. The rendered map is
first matched as an image to the footprint of the district's parcels and to the district outline, searching rotation
and scale, finding translation by FFT and scoring by Dice overlap. The fit is then refined by iterative closest point
from each parcel centroid to the nearest shape centroid.
\item \textbf{Classification.} Each parcel takes the category of the smallest shape containing its interior point.
\item \textbf{Checks.} A map is used only if (a) the median parcel-to-shape distance is at most 10 ft and (b) its plan
agrees with the existing use clearly more often than the same map shifted 300 ft. Because most land keeps its use in
a plan, a misregistered map fails this falsification test.
\end{enumerate}

Maps from @@phl_maps_used@@ of the @@phl_maps@@ plans passed, covering @@phl_districts_used@@ of @@phl_districts@@
districts and @@phl_share@@ of the city's parcels. Median registration errors were @@phl_err_lo@@--@@phl_err_hi@@ ft
(median @@phl_err_median@@ ft), less than the width of a typical rowhouse lot. The maps covered
@@phl_cov_lo@@--@@phl_cov_hi@@ of each district's parcels. The digitised plans agree with the existing use on
@@phl_agree_lo@@--@@phl_agree_hi@@ of parcels, against @@phl_shift_lo@@--@@phl_shift_hi@@ for the shifted maps, a
margin of at least @@phl_margin_min@@ points in every district. The maps of @@phl_not_used@@ also draw neighbouring
districts, which defeated the registration; those districts have no plan class. Philadelphia has no parcel history in
this study, so its plan layer serves the system but not the test below.

