\subsection{Parcel histories}

Seven cities have parcel histories that allow an out-of-time test (Table~\ref{tab:panels}). The definition of
development follows what each source records:
\begin{itemize}
\item a new building, or floor area up by more than 25\% and 2,000 sq ft, in Los Angeles (county assessor rolls,
2021--2025), Dallas (appraisal district files, 2022--2026) and New York (MapPLUTO, 2021--2025);
\item a new building in Milwaukee, where recorded floor area is not consistent across years;
\item a change to a built use of another group, or more homes, between the regional agencies' land-use inventories
in Phoenix and San Diego;
\item a new-construction permit issued in Chicago.
\end{itemize}
Lots already under construction at the start of a window are excluded, and every feature is measured at the start of
its window. The test windows contain @@test_lots@@ lots, of which @@test_developed@@ developed.

@@table_panels@@

\section{Methods}\label{sec:methods}

\paragraph{Feature blocks.} Four blocks are defined identically in every city, from portable classes and ratios
without city-specific codes:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{E, existing conditions:} the analysis group of the existing use, lot area, floor area, built floor-area
ratio, building age, homes and the land share of assessed value;
\item \textbf{C, context:} the share of lots within 400 m that were built or changed use in the five years before the
window, the number of lots within 400 m and their land-use mix;
\item \textbf{Z, zoning:} the zoning class, whether the existing use departs from it, and the step in housing
intensity from the existing use to the zoning class;
\item \textbf{P, adopted plan:} the same three features for the plan class.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Out-of-time ablation.} For each city and block set, a gradient-boosted classifier (LightGBM; 400 trees,
learning rate 0.04, 31 leaves) is fitted on the training window and scores the lots of the test window. The block
sets are E, E+C, E+C+Z, E+C+P and E+C+Z+P, together with the sets without context. We report the area under the ROC
curve (AUC), with average precision and precision among the top 1\% of lots in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:ap}. The gain
from adding a block is the difference in AUC; its 95\% interval comes from 200 bootstrap resamples of 2 km tiles,
which preserves spatial dependence. As a second view, all sets are also cross-fitted over five folds of 2 km tiles
within the full period.

\paragraph{Leakage control.} In Los Angeles both layers were compiled in 2019, before every window. New York and
Milwaukee have zoning from each year's records. In Phoenix, San Diego and Chicago the zoning or plan layer is current,
but zoning cases and plan polygons are dated. A conservative variant therefore excludes, in each window, lots whose
zoning or plan dates from the window's start or later. Dallas's current layers carry no dates, so its zoning and plan
gains are upper bounds.

\paragraph{Destination.} Among lots that developed in the test window, the class they became is predicted by the
existing use (no change), the zoning class, the plan class and models cross-fitted over tiles. Each is scored as one
of 16 classes and as one of nine groups.

\paragraph{Homes.} In the four cities with unit counts at both ends of the window, homes added by district are
compared with four projections:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{zoning build-out}, in which every lot reaches the homes per acre that the 90th percentile of lots of
its zoning class already reaches in the city;
\item \textbf{plan build-out}, the same for the plan class;
\item \textbf{likelihood-weighted build-out}, the same increments multiplied by each lot's out-of-time probability
of development;
\item \textbf{the trend}, homes completed in the five years before the window, scaled to its length.
\end{itemize}
We report the ratio of projected to observed total and the Spearman correlation across districts.

\paragraph{Transfer.} For the five cities whose outcome is a building or a permit, a model fitted on the other
cities' training windows scores the city's test window and is compared with the city's own model. A missing plan
layer is coded as unknown.

\paragraph{Manhattan rezonings.} To test the approach at the scale of a single decision, we replay three Manhattan
rezonings: East Harlem (2017), Inwood (2018) and SoHo/NoHo (2021).
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Sites} are lots that can add floor area under the adopted zoning.
\item \textbf{Capacity} assumes full build-out.
\item \textbf{The likelihood-weighted projection} multiplies each site's homes by its probability of redevelopment by
2025. The probability comes from a redevelopment model trained only on outcomes known by the adoption year, scored
with the adopted zoning in place.
\end{itemize}
That model was tested separately on Manhattan's annual records. Across @@ro_n@@ rolling origins (2016--2025, with
horizons of one, three and five years), its out-of-time AUC was @@ro_auc_lo@@--@@ro_auc_hi@@, above the best simple
baseline at every origin.

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Where development happens}

Existing conditions alone rank the lots that developed well (Table~\ref{tab:auc}). Out-of-time AUC with block E is
@@auc_E_rest_lo@@--@@auc_E_hi@@ in six cities and @@auc_E_lo@@ in San Diego, whose one-year window recorded few
changes.

@@table_auc@@

Context adds the most (Table~\ref{tab:gains}, Figure~\ref{fig:gains}). Its gain is significant in @@n_ctx_sig@@
cities (@@ctx_sig_cities@@), from +@@ctx_gain_lo@@ to +@@ctx_gain_hi@@. It is not significant in Milwaukee, and it is
negative in Phoenix (@@phx_ctx@@; interval @@phx_ctx_lo@@ to @@phx_ctx_hi@@). Phoenix's regional series starts in
2022, so recent change is unknown in its training window. Its context block therefore reduces to the number and
land-use mix of nearby lots, which did not generalise from the training to the test window.

@@table_gains@@

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.82\linewidth]{integration_layers.png}
\caption{Gain in out-of-time AUC from adding each block, with 95\% tile-bootstrap intervals.}
\label{fig:gains}
\end{figure}

Zoning carries information of its own. Added to existing conditions alone, it raises AUC significantly in
@@of_zE@@ cities (+@@zE_lo@@ to +@@zE_hi@@). Lots that developed were @@zdiff_ratio_lo@@ to
@@zdiff_ratio_hi@@ times as likely as other lots to have a zoning class outside their existing use group. Most of
this information is shared with existing conditions and context, however. Developed lots were also
@@vac_ratio_lo@@ to @@vac_ratio_hi@@ times as likely to be vacant, and once context is included the regulatory layers
add little:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{zoning} adds a significant gain over context in @@of_z@@ cities (@@z_sig_cities@@);
\item \textbf{the plan} adds one in @@of_p@@ (@@p_sig_cities@@);
\item outside Phoenix these gains are at most +@@zp_small_max@@;
\item in Los Angeles, where both layers predate the test, zoning and plan together add @@la_zp@@, which is not
significant.
\end{itemize}
@@neg_sentence@@

Phoenix is the exception: zoning adds +@@phx_z@@ and the plan +@@phx_p@@ over context, and its best block set,
@@phx_best_set@@ (AUC @@phx_best_auc@@), omits context. Development there, measured as a change of land-use class in
the regional inventory, is concentrated on vacant lots (@@phx_vac_dev@@ of developed lots against @@phx_vac_all@@ of
all lots). It is also concentrated on lots whose zoning or plan class differs from their existing use
(@@phx_zd_dev@@ and @@phx_pd_dev@@, against @@phx_zd_all@@ and @@phx_pd_all@@).

The conservative variant tests whether such gains come from layers updated during the study. @@cons_held@@
survive it. @@cons_lost@@ do not, so in those cases the apparent value of the layer came from lots whose zoning or
plan changed during the study. Spatial cross-fitting gives the same picture: context raises AUC in @@of_ctx_spatial@@
cities, and zoning on top of context raises it in @@of_z_spatial@@, by small amounts.

\subsection{What developed lots become}

The regulatory layers matter more for what a developing lot becomes (Table~\ref{tab:dest}). In @@of_dest_z@@ cities
with a destination, the zoning class names the new class correctly more often than the existing use does, by
@@dest_z_lo@@ to @@dest_z_hi@@ percentage points. (In Phoenix and San Diego, development is a change of class by
definition, so there the existing use is never right at class level.) In Dallas, whose plan was adopted in 2024, the plan class is right
for @@dal_plan_dest@@ of developed lots, against @@dal_zone_dest@@ for zoning and @@dal_none_dest@@ for no change. A
model combining the layers matches or beats the best single layer in @@of_model_layers@@ cities.

@@table_dest@@

\subsection{How many homes}

Capacity build-out overstates near-term housing by two orders of magnitude (Table~\ref{tab:homes}). Zoning and plan
build-out exceed the homes added in the test window @@bo_lo@@- to @@bo_hi@@-fold. Part of this gap is definitional,
since build-out is an end state rather than a two-year flow, but it shows how far capacity is from what a short
horizon delivers. Weighting build-out by the out-of-time likelihood brings the totals to @@lk_lo@@--@@lk_hi@@ times
the observed.

The likelihood-weighted projections rank districts less well than the five-year trend, however. Their Spearman
correlations are @@lk_rho_lo@@ to @@lk_rho_hi@@, against @@trend_rho_lo@@ to @@trend_rho_hi@@ for the trend, which
ranks better in @@of_trend@@ cities even though its totals are
@@trend_tot_lo@@--@@trend_tot_hi@@ times the observed. Recent local construction is the better guide to which
districts will add homes; likelihood-weighted capacity is the better guide to how many.

@@table_homes@@

\subsection{Transfer between cities}

A model fitted on other cities predicts less well than the city's own model in @@of_transfer@@ cities
(Table~\ref{tab:transfer}). With all blocks, the AUC is lower by @@transfer_gap_lo@@ to
@@transfer_gap_hi@@. The exception is @@transfer_better_city@@, which had only @@mke_dev@@ developed lots in its test
window and gains from the larger pooled sample. Context improves the transferred model in @@of_transfer_ctx@@ cities. Portable features carry much of the signal, but local history still matters.

@@table_transfer@@

\subsection{Manhattan rezonings}

The three rezonings show the same contrast at the scale of a single decision (Table~\ref{tab:replay},
Figure~\ref{fig:replay}). Capacity projected @@g_capacity@@ homes on development sites, and @@g_prorated@@ when
pro-rated to 2025. The likelihood-weighted projection gave @@g_likely@@; @@g_observed@@ had been built. The observed
count lies within the model's 95\% range in @@of_g@@ rezonings. SoHo/NoHo is below it, with
@@g_soho_built@@ homes built by 2025. Capacity describes full build-out by each review's build year, so the comparison
concerns the near term, the horizon for which the likelihood model is trained.

@@table_replay@@

