\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What the layers are for.} Across seven cities, zoning and the adopted plan add much less than existing
conditions and neighbourhood context to predicting \emph{where} development happens. They add more to predicting
\emph{what} it becomes, and they are needed to bound \emph{how much} it could hold. The pattern is consistent with the
causal literature. Zoning sets a ceiling and a direction, but whether a lot develops within a few years depends on its
current use, value and surroundings \citep{rosenthal1994,weber2006,charles2013}. Where upzoning did not change
construction in the short run \citep{freemark2020}, little predictive value is to be expected. Where it did, the
effect was strongest on vacant and underused parcels \citep{dong2024}, which existing conditions already identify.
Rezoning itself follows development opportunity \citep{gabbe2018}, which helps explain why two of the regulatory gains
vanished in the conservative variant. The plan results echo plan evaluation: plans mostly confirm existing use, so
their predictive information lies where they depart from it. Their conformance with outcomes should not be read as
influence \citep{berke2006,feitelson2017}.

\paragraph{Implications for planning support tools.} Tools that combine existing, permitted and planned layers
\citep{klosterman1999,geertman2020} should not assume that each layer improves a projection. The results suggest three
practices:
\begin{enumerate}
\item test each layer on the city's own history before relying on it, since its value varies by city and by question;
\item report likelihood-weighted projections beside capacity, which overstates near-term housing by orders of
magnitude;
\item report a trend baseline, which here ranked districts better than any model-based projection.
\end{enumerate}
These practices narrow the gap between claimed and demonstrated contributions that reviews of urban digital twins
describe \citep{lei2023,azadi2025}.

\paragraph{Reusable data and methods.} The crosswalks put ten cities' official codes on one schema as explicit
tables, and tests check that every source code is mapped. The digitisation shows that plans published as documents
need not stay outside quantitative analysis. Where the PDF is a vector export, a parcel layer can be recovered with
errors of a few feet, and the shifted-map control provides a falsification test that does not require the true plan.
The method relies only on a legend, filled shapes and a parcel layer, so it is not specific to Philadelphia. It
failed where a map also draws neighbouring districts.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The test covers @@test_lots@@ lots and @@test_developed@@ developments in seven large cities
with different regulatory systems. That is enough to show that the value of zoning and plans varies and is often
small, but not to estimate a general effect size. The main limitations are:
\begin{itemize}
\item the windows are short (one or two years) and, like the outcome definitions, differ by city;
\item plan and zoning are current layers in Dallas and in parts of Phoenix, San Diego and Chicago; the conservative
variant controls for this where dates exist, and in Dallas the gains are upper bounds;
\item Chicago's outcome is a permit, not a completed building;
\item context in Phoenix and San Diego lacks recent-change information in training;
\item the crosswalks were written by hand, and some existing-use layers are a few years older than the windows;
\item one model family is used, and AUC on rare events is complemented by average precision in the Appendix;
\item the homes comparison rests on four cities and the rezoning replay on three cases;
\item nothing here estimates what zoning or plans cause.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Zoning and adopted plans are the core of land-use regulation, and planning tools combine them with existing
conditions to project development. Tested on observed history in seven US cities, the two layers add little to
predicting where development happens once existing conditions and neighbourhood context are known. They help predict
what development becomes, and they bound how much a site could hold. Projecting at capacity overstated near-term
housing @@bo_lo@@- to @@bo_hi@@-fold. Weighting capacity by an out-of-time likelihood gave @@lk_lo@@--@@lk_hi@@ times
what was built, while a simple trend ranked districts better. The parcel layers for ten cities, the method for
digitising plans published as maps and the test protocol are open, so any city's layers can be tested the same way
before a tool relies on them.

\section*{Data and code availability}

The code, the result files behind every number, the figures and a manifest of every input (URL, retrieval date and
SHA-256) are in the PlanArena repository; \texttt{make reproduce} rebuilds the data and results from public sources.
Each city's ingestion report documents coverage, crosswalks and estimated fields. Owner and mailing fields in
assessor records were never requested or read.

